## Five relationships

Designing a program is largely deciding how its objects relate. Each relationship has a name, a meaning, and a natural way to write it in C++.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction LR
    Car *-- Engine : composition
    Department o-- Teacher : aggregation
    Doctor --> Patient : association
    Printer ..> Document : dependency
    Animal <|-- Dog : inheritance
```

| Relationship | Say it as | Does A control B's lifetime? | Typical code |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Composition** | A car **has an** engine, as a part | Yes: the part dies with the whole | A value member, or a `std::unique_ptr` |
| **Aggregation** | A department **has** teachers | No: they exist independently | A pointer, or a `std::weak_ptr` |
| **Association** | A doctor **treats** patients | No | Pointers or identifiers |
| **Dependency** | A printer **uses** a document | No: only during a call | A function parameter |
| **Inheritance** | A dog **is an** animal | | `class Dog : public Animal` (Milestone 8) |

The middle column is the one that matters for this milestone. It is the ownership question from [[7.02 new and delete — Dynamic Memory]], asked about members.

## Composition

The part belongs to the whole and has no life outside it.

```cpp
class Engine
{
public:
    void start() { std::cout << "vroom\n"; }
};

class Car
{
public:
    void drive() { m_engine.start(); }

private:
    Engine m_engine{};
    std::vector<Wheel> m_wheels{ 4 };
};
```

```
 ┌─────── Car ────────┐
 │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐  │     destroy the Car and the
 │ │Engine│ │Wheels│  │     Engine and Wheels go with it
 │ └──────┘ └──────┘  │
 └────────────────────┘
```

A value member is the simplest, fastest and safest form. Use a `std::unique_ptr` member only when you need what a pointer offers: the part may be absent, may be replaced, or is of a type chosen while running.

## Aggregation

The whole refers to parts that exist on their own and may outlive it.

```cpp
class Department
{
public:
    void add(Teacher& teacher) { m_teachers.push_back(&teacher); }

private:
    std::vector<Teacher*> m_teachers{};    // not owned
};

Teacher bob{ "Bob" };
{
    Department maths{};
    maths.add(bob);
}    // the Department is gone. Bob is unaffected.
```

The raw pointers here are observers, which is a legitimate use. The obligation runs the other way: the teachers must outlive the department's use of them, or the pointers dangle.

> [!note] A vector cannot hold references
> `std::vector<Teacher&>` does not compile, because a reference cannot be reassigned and a vector must be able to move its elements. Use pointers, or `std::vector<std::reference_wrapper<Teacher>>`.

## Association

Two independent objects that know about each other.

```cpp
class Patient;

class Doctor
{
public:
    void addPatient(Patient& patient);

private:
    std::vector<Patient*> m_patients{};
};

class Patient
{
private:
    std::vector<Doctor*> m_doctors{};
};
```

Links in both directions have to be kept consistent: adding a patient to a doctor must also add the doctor to the patient, and removal must undo both. Prefer a link in one direction, or store an identifier and look the object up when needed.

## Dependency

The lightest relationship. One class uses another for the length of a function call and keeps nothing.

```cpp
class Printer
{
public:
    void print(const Document& document);
};
```

## Choosing

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q1{"Is B a kind of A?"} -- yes --> I["Inheritance<br/>(Milestone 8)"]
    Q1 -- no --> Q2{"Is B a part of A that<br/>cannot exist without it?"}
    Q2 -- yes --> C["Composition<br/>value member or unique_ptr"]
    Q2 -- no --> Q3{"Does A keep a lasting<br/>link to B?"}
    Q3 -- yes --> A["Aggregation or association<br/>pointer, weak_ptr or identifier"]
    Q3 -- no --> D["Dependency<br/>function parameter"]
```

> [!tip] Prefer the weakest relationship that works
> A dependency is easier to reason about than a stored link, and composition is easier than inheritance. The well-known advice "favour composition over inheritance" is explained in [[8.06 Object Slicing, Casting and Composition]].

## Check yourself
```quiz
? What is the difference between composition and aggregation?
- Composition uses pointers; aggregation uses values
+ In composition the whole owns the part's lifetime; not so in aggregation
- Aggregation is when two classes inherit from each other
- There is none; they are two names for one idea
> A car's engine (composition) is created and destroyed with the car. A department's teachers (aggregation) exist on their own.

? Why can you not write `std::vector<Teacher&>`?
- Because `Teacher` is a class
- Because vectors can only hold built-in types
+ References cannot be reassigned, as elements must be
- Because references are always `const`
> Elements must be assignable objects. Store pointers or `std::reference_wrapper<Teacher>` instead.

? What is the simplest way to express composition?
- A raw pointer member created with `new`
- A `std::shared_ptr` member set in the constructor
- A global variable
+ A plain value member of the class
> A plain member is part of the object, so its lifetime is tied to the whole automatically.

? Why are links in both directions hard to maintain?
+ Every change must be made on both sides, and a missed update leaves the objects disagreeing
- Because C++ does not allow two classes to refer to each other
- Because they always cause memory leaks
- Because they make the program slower to compile
> A two-way link is two pieces of data that must stay consistent. Prefer one direction unless both are needed.
```
